---
import DeckLayout from '../../layouts/DeckLayout.astro';
import Slide from '../../components/slides/Slide.astro';
import Card from '../../components/slides/Card.astro';
import KeyTerms from '../../components/slides/KeyTerms.astro';
import Table from '../../components/slides/Table.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/slides/CodeBlock.astro';
import ThreeCanvas from '../../components/three/ThreeCanvas.astro';
import SpeakerNotes from '../../components/slides/SpeakerNotes.astro';

const minimalThreeJsCode = `import * as THREE from 'three';

// 1. Create Scene Graph & Perspective Camera
const scene = new THREE.Scene();
const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(
  75, 
  window.innerWidth / window.innerHeight, 
  0.1, 
  1000
);
camera.position.set(0, 1.5, 4);

// 2. Instantiate WebGL Renderer with sRGB Color Management
const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: true });
renderer.setSize(window.innerWidth, window.innerHeight);
renderer.setPixelRatio(Math.min(window.devicePixelRatio, 2));
renderer.outputColorSpace = THREE.SRGBColorSpace;
document.body.appendChild(renderer.domElement);

// 3. Construct Geometry, PBR Material & Mesh
const geometry = new THREE.TorusKnotGeometry(0.8, 0.25, 128, 32);
const material = new THREE.MeshStandardMaterial({
  color: 0x4338ca,
  roughness: 0.2,
  metalness: 0.8,
});
const mesh = new THREE.Mesh(geometry, material);
scene.add(mesh);

// 4. Add Directional Key Light & Ambient Fill
const keyLight = new THREE.DirectionalLight(0xffffff, 2.0);
keyLight.position.set(5, 5, 5);
scene.add(keyLight);
scene.add(new THREE.AmbientLight(0xffffff, 0.4));

// 5. Delta-Timed Animation Loop
const clock = new THREE.Clock();
function animate() {
  requestAnimationFrame(animate);
  const delta = clock.getDelta();
  mesh.rotation.x += 0.5 * delta;
  mesh.rotation.y += 0.8 * delta;
  renderer.render(scene, camera);
}
animate();

// 6. Handle Viewport Resizing
window.addEventListener('resize', () => {
  camera.aspect = window.innerWidth / window.innerHeight;
  camera.updateProjectionMatrix();
  renderer.setSize(window.innerWidth, window.innerHeight);
});`;

const animationLoopCode = `const clock = new THREE.Clock();

function renderLoop() {
  requestAnimationFrame(renderLoop);

  // Obtain elapsed delta in seconds since last frame
  const delta = clock.getDelta();

  // Rotate mesh at a constant 45 degrees (0.785 rad) per second
  cubeMesh.rotation.y += (Math.PI / 4) * delta;
  cubeMesh.rotation.x += (Math.PI / 8) * delta;

  // Dispatch draw calls for the updated scene hierarchy
  renderer.render(scene, camera);
}
renderLoop();`;
---

<DeckLayout
  title="CS 3840: Computer Graphics: Introduction to Three.js & WebGL"
  description="Full 1-hour university lecture notes on WebGL pipeline, Three.js architecture, spatial math, shading models, and GPU optimization."
>
  <!-- Slide 1: Course Title & Pedagogical Scope -->
  <Slide
    id="title-slide"
    eyebrow="CS 3840 · COMPUTER GRAPHICS & GPU ARCHITECTURE"
    title="Three.js Core Architecture & the WebGL Pipeline"
    subtitle="Lecture 06 · Scene Graph, Spatial Mathematics, PBR Illumination & GPU Buffers"
    unit="Unit 01: Three.js Fundamentals"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4 max-w-4xl">
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        Modern web browsers interface with local graphics hardware through WebGL (and WebGPU), an OpenGL ES derivative that operates as a low-level, state-driven rasterization pipeline. Because raw WebGL requires hundreds of lines of boilerplate code to compile GLSL shaders, bind Vertex Buffer Objects (VBOs), and configure matrix uniforms, production applications rely on <strong>Three.js</strong> as a high-level scene graph abstraction layer.
      </p>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        This lecture deconstructs how Three.js maps high-level object hierarchies onto low-level GPU draw calls. We examine the core architectural trinity (Scene, Camera, Renderer), spatial coordinate mathematics and quaternion rotations, geometry buffer memory layouts, physically based rendering (PBR) materials, and frame-rate-independent animation scheduling.
      </p>
      <div class="p-4 rounded-lg bg-indigo-50 border-2 border-indigo-200 mt-4">
        <h4 class="text-xs font-bold text-indigo-950 uppercase tracking-wide mb-1">Pedagogical Objective</h4>
        <p class="text-sm text-indigo-900 m-0 font-semibold">
          Master the full lifecycle of a 3D browser application: from constructing memory-efficient BufferGeometries and configuring light matrices, to writing bug-free render loops that avoid GPU memory leaks and maintain 60 FPS across heterogeneous client hardware.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Welcome students to Lecture 06. Emphasize that Three.js is not a separate rendering engine from WebGL, but rather a structured scene graph and asset manager that compiles down to WebGL state commands and draw calls.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- Slide 2: WebGL State Machine vs Three.js Scene Graph -->
  <Slide
    id="webgl-vs-threejs"
    eyebrow="01 · FOUNDATIONS & ABSTRACTIONS"
    title="The WebGL State Machine vs. Three.js Scene Graph"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="Unit 01: Three.js Fundamentals"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 h-full">
      <!-- Main Explanation -->
      <div class="md:col-span-2 space-y-4">
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Raw WebGL operates strictly as an immediate-mode state machine: you bind a shader program, bind vertex attribute arrays, upload uniform matrices, and issue a draw command (<code>gl.drawElements</code>). WebGL maintains no memory of "3D objects" or spatial parent-child relationships once a draw call completes.
        </p>
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          <strong>Three.js implements a retained-mode Scene Graph</strong>: a hierarchical N-ary tree where every node is an instance of <code>THREE.Object3D</code> (meshes, lights, cameras, groups). Each node maintains its own local transformation matrix (T_local = T · R · S). During each frame, Three.js traverses the tree from root to leaves, multiplying local matrices by parent matrices to compute the final World Transformation Matrix (M_world) passed to the GPU.
        </p>
        <div class="lecture-card border-2 border-slate-300">
          <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-950 mb-1 uppercase">Key Architectural Benefit</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-900 m-0 leading-relaxed font-medium">
            Transforming a parent node (e.g. rotating a car chassis) automatically rotates all child nodes (wheels, headlights) without requiring manual matrix math for every individual sub-mesh.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Key Terms Sidebar -->
      <div class="md:col-span-1">
        <KeyTerms
          title="Key Terms"
          terms={[
            {
              term: "Scene Graph",
              definition: "A hierarchical tree data structure where parent spatial transformations automatically cascade down to all child nodes."
            },
            {
              term: "State Machine",
              definition: "A software architecture (like raw WebGL/OpenGL) where drawing behavior is determined by currently bound global state."
            },
            {
              term: "Draw Call",
              definition: "A CPU command instructing the GPU to render a specific batch of triangles using currently bound buffers and shaders."
            },
            {
              term: "World Matrix",
              definition: "The accumulated 4x4 transformation matrix that defines an object's absolute position, rotation, and scale in global 3D space."
            }
          ]}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Explain how tree traversal works. Highlight that calling `scene.add(child)` reparents the node. If an object is not in the scene graph, it will never receive a draw call.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- Slide 3: The Fundamental Trinity -->
  <Slide
    id="core-trinity"
    eyebrow="02 · CORE SUBSYSTEMS"
    title="The Core Trinity: Scene, Camera & WebGLRenderer"
    layout="card-grid"
    unit="Unit 01: Three.js Fundamentals"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-5 my-auto">
      <Card title="1. THREE.Scene" badge="World Graph" glow="indigo">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-2">Role: Universal spatial container & coordinate origin (0,0,0).</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Acts as the root node of the scene graph. Holds all renderable meshes, light sources, camera rigs, and background environments. It also manages global scene properties like background colors and atmospheric depth fog (<code>scene.fog</code>).
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="2. PerspectiveCamera" badge="Viewing Frustum" glow="cyan">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-2">Role: Converts 3D world coordinates into 2D Normalized Device Coordinates (NDC).</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Simulates human vision using a geometric frustum defined by four parameters: Field of View (FOV in degrees), Aspect Ratio (width/height), Near Clipping Plane, and Far Clipping Plane. Everything outside this pyramid is clipped.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="3. WebGLRenderer" badge="Rasterization Engine" glow="amber">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-2">Role: Executes GPU shader pipelines & writes pixels to HTML5 Canvas.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Manages the WebGL context, color space management (<code>SRGBColorSpace</code>), hardware pixel ratio scaling (<code>renderer.setPixelRatio</code>), depth buffer testing, and invokes <code>renderer.render(scene, camera)</code> every frame.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Walk students through each card. Emphasize that the camera must be positioned away from origin (e.g. z = 5), because objects default to (0,0,0).
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- Slide 4: Coordinate Spaces & Quaternions -->
  <Slide
    id="coordinate-math"
    eyebrow="03 · SPATIAL MATHEMATICS"
    title="Coordinate Systems & Euler vs. Quaternion Rotations"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="Unit 01: Three.js Fundamentals"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 h-full">
      <div class="md:col-span-2 space-y-4">
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Three.js uses a <strong>Right-Handed Cartesian Coordinate System</strong>: positive $X$ points right, positive $Y$ points vertically up, and positive $Z$ points directly toward the viewer out of the screen.
        </p>
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          While intuitive, <strong>Euler angle rotations</strong> (specifying yaw, pitch, and roll in degrees/radians) suffer from two major mathematical limitations: rotation order dependency (rotating X then Y then Z yields different orientations than Z then Y then X) and <strong>Gimbal Lock</strong>, which is the loss of one rotational degree of freedom when two rotation axes align parallel to each other.
        </p>
        <div class="lecture-card border-2 border-slate-300">
          <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-950 mb-1 uppercase">The Quaternion Solution</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-900 m-0 leading-relaxed font-medium">
            Three.js uses 4-dimensional <strong>Unit Quaternions</strong> ($q = xi + yj + zk + w$) under the hood for all rotations. Quaternions prevent gimbal lock completely and enable smooth, artifact-free spherical linear interpolation (<strong>SLERP</strong>) between 3D orientations.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="md:col-span-1">
        <KeyTerms
          title="Spatial Terms"
          terms={[
            {
              term: "Right-Handed System",
              definition: "Standard 3D orientation where +X is right, +Y is up, and +Z points out toward the viewer."
            },
            {
              term: "Gimbal Lock",
              definition: "A mathematical breakdown in Euler rotations where two axes align, reducing 3D rotation to a 2D plane."
            },
            {
              term: "Unit Quaternion",
              definition: "A 4D algebraic vector [x, y, z, w] representing 3D spatial rotation without axis singularities."
            },
            {
              term: "SLERP",
              definition: "Spherical Linear Interpolation: the standard algorithm for smoothly animating between two 3D quaternion orientations."
            }
          ]}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Demonstrate the right-hand rule with your hand: thumb = X, index = Y, middle finger = Z. Explain how aircraft navigation and camera orbital controls require quaternions to prevent sudden spinning snaps.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- Slide 5: BufferGeometry & Memory Layout -->
  <Slide
    id="geometry-buffers"
    eyebrow="04 · GEOMETRY ENGINE"
    title="BufferGeometry: VRAM Allocation & Attribute Layout"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="Unit 01: Three.js Fundamentals"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 h-full">
      <div class="md:col-span-2 space-y-4">
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          In Three.js, all 3D mesh surfaces are constructed from triangle primitives represented by <code>THREE.BufferGeometry</code>. Unlike legacy object-per-vertex designs, BufferGeometry stores all vertex data inside continuous typed arrays (<code>Float32Array</code>) that are copied directly into GPU Video RAM (VRAM) as <strong>Vertex Buffer Objects (VBOs)</strong>.
        </p>
        <div class="lecture-card border-2 border-slate-300 space-y-2">
          <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-950 uppercase">Essential Buffer Attributes:</h4>
          <ul class="text-xs text-slate-900 space-y-1.5 pl-4 list-disc font-medium">
            <li><strong>position (3 floats per vertex)</strong>: $[x, y, z]$ coordinates defining vertex location in local model space.</li>
            <li><strong>normal (3 floats per vertex)</strong>: Unit perpendicular vectors $[n_x, n_y, n_z]$ required by lighting equations to compute surface reflection and shading.</li>
            <li><strong>uv (2 floats per vertex)</strong>: Texture coordinates $[u, v]$ mapping 2D bitmap images across 3D triangular faces.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-800 font-medium">
          <strong>Indexed Geometry Optimization:</strong> Using <code>geometry.setIndex()</code> with a <code>Uint16Array</code> or <code>Uint32Array</code> allows multiple adjacent triangles to share common vertices, cutting GPU memory consumption by up to $66\%$.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="md:col-span-1">
        <KeyTerms
          title="Memory Terms"
          terms={[
            {
              term: "VBO",
              definition: "Vertex Buffer Object: contiguous GPU memory buffer storing vertex positions, normals, and UVs."
            },
            {
              term: "Surface Normal",
              definition: "A normalized vector perpendicular to a triangle surface, vital for calculating light reflections."
            },
            {
              term: "UV Mapping",
              definition: "2D coordinate system [0.0 to 1.0] defining how 2D texture pixels map onto 3D polygon vertices."
            },
            {
              term: "Indexed Drawing",
              definition: "Reusing vertex buffer entries via index lists (gl.drawElements) rather than duplicating identical vertices."
            }
          ]}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Stress why Float32Array is mandatory: JavaScript garbage collection is bypassed during rendering because the array buffer is pinned and uploaded straight to the GPU driver.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- Slide 6: Material Comparison Table -->
  <Slide
    id="material-models"
    eyebrow="05 · SHADING & ILLUMINATION"
    title="Material Shading Models: Capabilities & Performance"
    layout="table"
    unit="Unit 01: Three.js Fundamentals"
  >
    <div class="my-auto">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-900 font-medium mb-3">
        Choosing the right material model balances visual fidelity against GPU fragment shader execution cost across target devices:
      </p>

      <Table
        headers={["Material Class", "Illumination Model", "Light Interaction", "Specular Highlights", "GPU Compute Cost"]}
        rows={[
          ["MeshBasicMaterial", "Flat / Unlit", "Zero (Ignores all scene lights)", "None", "Minimal (Fastest)"],
          ["MeshLambertMaterial", "Lambertian Diffuse", "Diffuse reflections only (Cosine law)", "None (Matte appearance)", "Low (Per-vertex shading)"],
          ["MeshPhongMaterial", "Blinn-Phong", "Diffuse + Specular shininess highlights", "Yes (Configurable shininess)", "Medium (Per-pixel specular)"],
          ["MeshStandardMaterial", "Physically Based (PBR)", "Metallic-Roughness energy conservation", "Yes (Photorealistic reflection)", "High (Standard for 3D web)"],
          ["MeshPhysicalMaterial", "Advanced PBR", "Adds Clearcoat, Transmission & Sheen", "Yes (Glass, Car Paint, Gems)", "Highest (Heavy shader)"]
        ]}
      />

      <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 font-semibold mt-3">
        *Recommendation: Use <strong>MeshStandardMaterial</strong> as default for modern applications; drop to <strong>MeshLambertMaterial</strong> for low-end mobile devices.
      </p>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Explain energy conservation in PBR: a surface cannot reflect more light than it receives. Contrast Lambert's matte look with Standard's realistic metallic roughness reflections.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- Slide 7: Lighting Subsystems & Shadow Maps -->
  <Slide
    id="lighting-systems"
    eyebrow="06 · ILLUMINATION & SHADOWS"
    title="Light Sources & Two-Pass Shadow Map Computation"
    layout="card-grid"
    unit="Unit 01: Three.js Fundamentals"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-5 my-auto">
      <Card title="Directional Light" badge="Sunlight / Infinite" glow="indigo">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-2">Parallel rays emitted from an infinitely distant source.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Light rays travel parallel in a single vector direction with zero distance attenuation. Used for simulating sunlight. Uses an <code>OrthographicCamera</code> projection box to generate parallel directional shadow maps.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Point & SpotLight" badge="Localized / Conical" glow="cyan">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-2">Radial spherical emission or focused conical spotlights.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Emits light radially in all directions from a point in space with physical inverse-square intensity falloff (I = I_0 / d²). Spotlights constrain emission within an angular cone with configurable penumbra softness.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Shadow Map Pipeline" badge="Two-Pass Rendering" glow="amber">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-2">How real-time dynamic shadows are calculated on the GPU.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Pass 1: Render the scene from the light's perspective into a depth texture (Shadow Map). Pass 2: During main camera rendering, compare each pixel's depth against the shadow map to determine if it is obscured.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Walk students through the shadow map pipeline: enable `renderer.shadowMap.enabled = true`, mark meshes with `castShadow = true` and `receiveShadow = true`.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- Slide 8: Animation Lifecycle & Clock Delta -->
  <Slide
    id="animation-loop"
    eyebrow="07 · RUNTIME EXECUTION"
    title="The Render Loop & Frame-Rate Independence"
    layout="code"
    unit="Unit 01: Three.js Fundamentals"
  >
    <div class="my-auto">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-900 font-medium mb-2">
        To animate a 3D scene, schedule an execution loop synchronized with the browser's display refresh rate using <code>requestAnimationFrame</code> and scale transformations by elapsed clock delta:
      </p>

      <CodeBlock
        title="renderLoop.js: Frame-Rate Independent Animation"
        lang="javascript"
        code={animationLoopCode}
        output={"[Renderer] V-Sync loop active: 60.0 FPS | Clock Delta: 0.0166s"}
        pitfalls={[
          "Rotating by fixed constants (mesh.rotation.y += 0.05) causes objects to spin 2.4x faster on 144Hz monitors than on 60Hz screens.",
          "Instantiating geometries or materials inside the render loop causes catastrophic GPU memory leaks and garbage collection stutters.",
          "Using setInterval or setTimeout instead of requestAnimationFrame wastes battery and causes severe visual frame tearing."
        ]}
      />
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Emphasize the delta multiplier: `rotation += speed * delta`. Show why 144Hz vs 60Hz will behave identically only when multiplying by delta time in seconds.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- Slide 9: Complete Integrated Program -->
  <Slide
    id="complete-code"
    eyebrow="08 · COMPLETE IMPLEMENTATION"
    title="Complete Runnable Three.js Application"
    layout="code"
    unit="Unit 01: Three.js Fundamentals"
  >
    <div class="my-auto">
      <p class="text-xs text-slate-900 font-medium mb-1">
        A production-grade, complete TypeScript program setting up the Scene Graph, Camera Frustum, PBR Mesh, Directional Lighting, Window Resize Handling, and Render Loop:
      </p>

      <CodeBlock
        title="main.ts: Complete Standalone Application"
        lang="typescript"
        lines="3-11|13-18|20-28|30-35|37-46|48-53"
        code={minimalThreeJsCode}
        output={"WebGL context initialized successfully. Active Scene: 1 Mesh, 2 Lights. Rendering at 60 FPS."}
        pitfalls={[
          "Forgetting to add camera to scene is fine, but failing to add mesh (scene.add(mesh)) results in an invisible object.",
          "Leaving camera at (0, 0, 0) places it inside the mesh origin, rendering a blank screen.",
          "Omitting camera.updateProjectionMatrix() on resize causes stretched or compressed distortion."
        ]}
      />
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Step through lines: 3-11 (Scene & Camera), 13-18 (Renderer), 20-28 (Geometry & PBR Material), 30-35 (Lighting), 37-46 (Animation Loop), 48-53 (Resize handler).
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- Slide 10: Live Embedded Interactive 3D Demonstration -->
  <Slide
    id="live-demo"
    eyebrow="09 · INTERACTIVE DEMONSTRATION"
    title="Live Interactive 3D Mesh: Vertex Normals & PBR Shading"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="Unit 01: Three.js Fundamentals"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 items-center my-auto">
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          The interactive model on the right is a live <code>THREE.TorusKnotGeometry</code> rendered in real-time inside this slide using <code>MeshStandardMaterial</code> with metallic-roughness properties.
        </p>
        <div class="lecture-card border-2 border-slate-300">
          <h4 class="text-xs font-bold text-indigo-950 uppercase tracking-wide mb-1">Classroom Interaction</h4>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-900 m-0 leading-relaxed font-medium">
            <strong>Click and drag</strong> directly on the 3D model with your mouse to rotate and inspect vertex normal illumination, specular highlight shifts, and ambient occlusion in real-time.
          </p>
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 font-bold m-0">
          *Notice how light highlights glide over the surface based on the dot product between the light vector and vertex normal ($N \cdot L$).
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="flex justify-center items-center">
        <ThreeCanvas model="knot" color="#4338ca" height="320px" />
      </div>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Demonstrate clicking and dragging the 3D model live. Explain how the specular highlight moves as you rotate the torus knot geometry relative to the fixed directional light.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- Slide 11: Production Optimization -->
  <Slide
    id="gpu-optimization"
    eyebrow="10 · PERFORMANCE OPTIMIZATION"
    title="GPU Memory Management & Draw Call Optimization"
    layout="card-grid"
    unit="Unit 01: Three.js Fundamentals"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-5 my-auto">
      <Card title="Explicit Resource Disposal" badge="VRAM Leaks" glow="rose">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-2">JavaScript Garbage Collector cannot free GPU Video Memory.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Removing an object from the scene (<code>scene.remove(mesh)</code>) only removes the scene graph pointer. You MUST call <code>geometry.dispose()</code>, <code>material.dispose()</code>, and <code>texture.dispose()</code> to deallocate buffers from GPU VRAM.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="InstancedMesh" badge="10,000+ Objects" glow="cyan">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-2">Batching thousands of identical meshes into a single draw call.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Creating 5,000 individual meshes creates 5,000 separate CPU-to-GPU draw calls, causing severe frame drops. <code>THREE.InstancedMesh</code> passes a single shared geometry and material with an array of transformation matrices in 1 draw call.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Texture MIP Mapping" badge="Power of Two" glow="indigo">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-2">Texture dimension rules and GPU texture compression.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Always size textures to Powers of Two ($512\times512$, $1024\times1024$, $2048\times2048$) so the GPU can generate pre-filtered MIP map downsamples. Use modern compressed GPU texture formats (KTX2 / Basis Universal) to cut VRAM footprint by 75%.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Explain why single-page applications crash on mobile after navigating back and forth between 3D routes if disposal is omitted: the GPU VRAM runs out and the browser context is lost.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- Slide 12: Lecture Synthesis & Conclusion -->
  <Slide
    id="summary-synthesis"
    eyebrow="11 · LECTURE SYNTHESIS"
    title="Summary & Conceptual Pipeline: From Code to Pixels"
    unit="Unit 01: Three.js Fundamentals"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4 max-w-4xl my-auto">
